Building openboot.bin
=====================

OpenBoot is Sun's implementation of IEEE 1275-1994, Open Firmware:
https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1275/1932/

    make obp          TOOLCHAIN=sun on Solaris/SPARC, TOOLCHAIN=gnu on GNU/Linux
    make obp-verify   gnu only: the equivalence check against Sun's own images

Output: $(OUTDIR)/openboot.bin. Both paths build the same Sun sources, apply
the same patches/obp/forth/*.patch, and produce equivalent images.

Sun's makefiles build in place, so the build copies src/obp to
$(WORKDIR)/sun4v-qemu-kit and works there.


Requirements
------------
sun path (the reference):
  - Solaris/SPARC. The host tools and the target's decomp.o share one
    compiler, so this is a native build.
  - Sun Studio. Default SUN_STUDIO=/opt/SUNWspro.
  - /usr/ccs/bin/make, the SysV make. NOT gmake: these makefiles use
    `VAR:sh =` and target-local `target := VAR +=`. gmake does not fail
    loudly here, it mis-parses.

gnu path: the sparc64-linux-gnu cross toolchain with its -m32 multilib, and
the qemu-sparc64 and qemu-sparc32plus linux-user emulators. Full list in
REQUIREMENTS.LINUX. Driver: build/obp/gnu/build-obp.sh, called by `make obp`.


Why the build host must run SPARC code
--------------------------------------
The Forth engine is portable C, but the dictionary it loads is native SPARC
machine code, entered as a function pointer: each word ends in real SPARC
instructions, called directly, not interpreted. So whatever runs the build
must execute SPARC. Everything else in src/obp is built from source,
including the engine and every host tool.

The .dic files in src/obp are not sourceless either. Sun's build metacompiles
six of the seven from obp/fm/kernel and obp/fm/meta on every run
(fm/kernel/sparc/depend.mk), starting from one shipped root,
obp/os/sun/sparc/forth.dic:

    forth.dic -> kernel-xref.dic -> forth32-xref.dic -> k32t32-xref.dic
              -> f32t32.dic -> k64t32.dic, and f32t32.dic -> tokenize.dic

The whole chain, from forth.dic to openboot.bin with the tools, the two engines
and the dropins, is drawn in docs/obp-build-flow.png.

The 32-bit kernel is built twice in that chain, once by the shipped root and
once by a host rebuilt around the new kernel. The two are identical: the
kernel reproduces itself. Both paths run this chain from the same root, so
forth.dic is the one shipped binary either path reads.

os/unix/simforth/ looks like a way around this -- a SPARC simulator instead
of the real thing -- but only a stray .fth file and a depend.mk survive in
the release, no simulator source.


The gnu path
------------
Emulation, not portability. The engine (wrapper.c, fsys.c) and the host
tools that write the OBMD headers (makedi, makeprom, libchksum) are
cross-built static for sparc64-linux and run under QEMU linux-user, which
executes the dictionary's SPARC code on an x86 host.

The engine is built twice, and each runs the steps Sun's build runs on it:

    forth32  -m32, under qemu-sparc32plus: builds every dictionary from
             forth.dic (build/obp/gnu/tools/bootstrap-dics.sh), then the
             tokenizer, fonts and logo
    forth64  under qemu-sparc64: bootprom.bin, stand.dic, reset.o

There is no sparc-linux-gnu triplet; the 32-bit engine comes from the sparc64
cross gcc's -m32 multilib and Ubuntu's 32-bit SPARC glibc
(gcc-13-multilib-sparc64-linux-gnu, libc6-dev-sparc-sparc64-cross).

Sun's own dropins.mk and every per-dropin depend.mk are used unchanged; only
the dmake layer (common.mk) is reimplemented, in
build/obp/gnu/obp-gnu{,-inner}.mk. Every substitution, with why it is needed
and how it was shown equivalent, is listed in tests/full/obp/TESTS. Patches
needed only by this path are in patches/obp/gnu/; they do not change the
product.

What still differs from the sun path's output:
  - decomp.o's code: a different compiler, same behaviour on every real
    payload;
  - addresses of the engine binary saved inside the Forth images stand.dic
    and bootprom.bin. The PROM re-initialises them at cold start.


Checking the result: make obp-verify
------------------------------------
Seven checks (tests/full/obp/internal/*.sh, run in order by
tests/full/obp/run), entirely on the gnu path, no tarball or Solaris host
needed. The first (src-intact.sh) is not OBP-specific -- it checks
PRISTINE-MANIFEST against the whole working tree, src/hypervisor included --
the other six are:

  1. The three kernels a pci build makes from forth.dic are byte-identical
     to the copies Sun shipped in src/obp, and their six word listings match
     Sun's line for line (dic-kernels.sh).
  2. A second bootstrap generation, using the first generation's own output
     as the root instead of Sun's, reproduces both itself and the first
     generation exactly (dic-fixpoint.sh).
  3. The host dictionaries the build actually runs (forth32-xref.dic,
     f32t32.dic) reconcile against Sun's shipped copies once the known,
     already-understood differences are accounted for -- a shift from one
     assembler word Sun's shipped copies lack, and the engine-binary-address
     class (dic-host.sh, tests/full/obp/lib/dic-diff.py).
  4. Pristine builds (none of patches/obp/forth) of the two targets Sun
     shipped an image of, niagara2/release and niagara/t1_fpga, are walked
     record by record: every record except OBP (decomp.o) and bootprom must
     match Sun's byte for byte. The expected hashes are in
     tests/full/obp/expected/sun-records.sha256, taken from the OpenSPARC T2
     release archive (sun-records.sh).
  5. A pristine build and the patched (product) build differ only in the
     dropins this project's own patches touch, nothing else
     (patch-locality.sh).
  6. The word list of the pci build's stand.dic is compared with
     tests/full/obp/expected/dict-dump.sha256 (dict-content.sh).

`make obp-test-external TARBALL=<OpenSPARCT2_Arch.1.2.tar.bz2>` additionally
confirms src/obp and src/hypervisor are what Sun's release archive actually
contains (needs the tarball; not part of the gate above).
`tests/full/obp/external/sun-records-regenerate.sh <tarball>` rewrites both
committed hash files above; needed only after src/obp changes, review the
diff before committing.

tests/full/obp/TESTS has the details and the evidence behind each claim.


Three quirks in Sun's makefiles
-------------------------------
build/obp.mk (sun path) works around all three. The gnu path never runs
Sun's dmake layer; obp-gnu.mk handles 2 and 3 in its own rules.

1. dropins.mk must exist before the first make. arch/common.mk includes it
   and also generates it, but Sun make treats a missing include as fatal and
   will not remake it first. An empty one is seeded.

2. decomp.o needs its own make invocation. DECOMPSIZE runs `size -f decomp.o`
   when the makefile is parsed, so on a clean tree it expands empty and
   reset.o compiles with a malformed constant.

3. reset.bin must be padded before makeprom reads it. Its header counts .bss,
   which occupies no file bytes, so the record is short and the dropin chain
   breaks at its first link. See build/obp/pad-reset-bin.sh.

Quirk 3 produces no output at all -- not one character -- so every build
walks the finished chain (build/obp/verify-dropin-chain.sh) and refuses an
image whose records do not end exactly at EOF.


Which image is running
----------------------
QEMU searches every -L directory and will pick up an openboot.bin you did not
mean. Both paths stamp the image the same way, from build/stamp.sh: the
release string is 4.x.build_122+qemu where Sun's says
4.x.build_122***PROTOTYPE BUILD***, and 4.x.build_122+qemu-0.1 when built
from a clean checkout of tag v0.1 (OBP_FIRMWAREREL overrides it). The
sub-release carries the date and commit. At ok:

    ok cd /openprom
    ok .properties
    version    OBP 4.x.build_122+qemu 2026/09/28 23:05
    model      SUNW,4.x.build_122+qemu[ sun4v-qemu-kit 09/28/26 23:05 git <rev> #0]

The date is not the time of the build. It is SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH if set, else
the commit date of a clean tree, in UTC, so two builds of one commit give the
same openboot.bin (and the same q.bin, which takes its date from the same
build/stamp.sh). Only a tree with uncommitted changes -- "-dirty" after the
hash -- stamps the build time instead.

`#0` at the end is Sun's build counter: Sun's makefile raised it with every
rebuild in one tree. Here it is always 0, so that two builds of one commit stay
byte-identical.

On the sun path the stamp reaches Sun's makefiles as command-line macros
(SUB-RELEASE, FIRMWAREREL, DATEFMT; build/obp.mk), which override their own,
so release.mk's logname and workspace name no longer appear.

Do not use `.compile-date`: it has no name in the dictionary.

Niagara2 images have dropins that T1 images do not: niu-nexus, niu-network.

A normal build is never byte-identical to Sun's image: the recipe stamps the
build date and increments a counter every time.
